verdict.refinedFix:
In renderNowBanner, leave the hint out of the shape and patch it in place. Tested in a scratch copy (all 436 tests pass; the DOM equals a full rebuild after every step in all three layouts; the bar node is kept):
```js
const WX_RE = /<div class="now-wx">[\s\S]*?<\/div>/;
const wxMatch = html ? WX_RE.exec(html) : null;
const shape = (html || '').replace(WX_RE, '').replace(/(<jelly-progress[^>]*?)value="\d+"/, '$1');
const bar = el.querySelector && el.querySelector('jelly-progress.now-progress');
if (html && bar && el.__nowShape === shape) {
  const m = /<jelly-progress[^>]*?value="(\d+)"/.exec(html);
  if (m && bar.getAttribute('value') !== m[1]) bar.setAttribute('value', m[1]);
  const wxEl = el.querySelector('.now-wx');
  if (!wxMatch) { if (wxEl) wxEl.remove(); }
  else if (!wxEl || wxEl.outerHTML !== wxMatch[0]) {
    const t = document.createElement('template'); t.innerHTML = wxMatch[0];
    const fresh = t.content.firstElementChild;
    if (wxEl) wxEl.replaceWith(fresh); else (el.querySelector('.now-body') || el).appendChild(fresh);
  }
  return;
}
```
`.now-wx` is the last child of `.now-body` in the main and elective layouts, so appendChild matches a full rebuild. The stale el.__html left by the in-place patch is safe, because setHtmlIfChanged also compares el.__dom to the live innerHTML and will rewrite. Do not try to keep the bar across the live-board hide: the banner genuinely reappears. A remote team rename changes mealCleanupNote text and would still re-create the bar (rare, acceptable).

Split the per-element loop out of tickBoardClocks and call it at the end of renderAll, so every rebuilt board (home board, game view, tally board) is born in its final state. Tested in a scratch copy: classes, colour, heartbeat and glow are intact right after the snapshot on home and game view, for viewer and editor.
```js
function paintBoardClocks() {
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-game-clock]').forEach((el) => { /* the existing loop body */ });
}
function tickBoardClocks() { const settled = settleExpiredClocks(); if (wakeLockWanted && !anyTimerRunning()) releaseWakeLock(); if (settled) renderAll(); paintBoardClocks(); }
// last line of renderAll():
paintBoardClocks();
```
Do not call tickBoardClocks() itself from renderAll: it runs settleExpiredClocks (writes) and can recurse through renderAll. The CSS animations restart from phase 0 on the new nodes, but they are born with the right class, so only the 1 s heartbeat phase resets, which is imperceptible. setHtmlIfChanged cannot be used for the board with a running clock: the tick mutates the DOM, so its el.__dom guard always fails.

Skip the rebuild when nothing but the ticking clock changed. Tested in a scratch copy: same node after renderAll, pulse phase 2450, 2866, 3283 ... advancing as a steady ring should, tracker score change still rebuilds, game ending still hides the wrapper, a new game rebuilds, the clock node is kept and still ticks.
```js
const liveHtml = boardHTML + games.map(...).join('');   // was: wrap.innerHTML = ...
const liveKey = liveHtml.replace(/(data-game-clock[^>]*>)[^<]*/g, '$1'); // clock text is the tick's
if (wrap.__liveKey === liveKey && wrap.firstElementChild) return;
wrap.__liveKey = liveKey;
wrap.innerHTML = liveHtml;
// ...then the existing listener wiring + boardDiffCelebrate
```
Also set `wrap.__liveKey = null` in the empty-games early return. Do NOT use setHtmlIfChanged here: its el.__dom === el.innerHTML guard fails whenever tickBoardClocks changed a clock digit or boardCelebrate appended particles, so it would rebuild every time. Listeners need no change: they are attached only on the write that creates the nodes, and openGame looks the game up by id at click time, so no stale closure. The cards contain no jelly components and no user-toggled attributes.

Use a quiet flag around the settle write. Tested in a scratch copy: editor peak 0 particles, the clock still stops, and tap-driven celebrations are unaffected.
```js
let joyQuiet = false;
function joyCelebrate() { if (joyQuiet) return; /* existing body */ }
// settleExpiredClocks:
joyQuiet = true;
try { setClock(g, (cl) => { cl.running = false; cl.remaining = 0; }); } finally { joyQuiet = false; }
```
This is more precise than the proposed 'only after a recent pointer event' rule, which also suppresses keyboard-driven saves.

Save the selection and restore focus whether or not there is a draft. Tested in a scratch copy: caret 7,7 kept and focus kept in both cases.
```js
const selStart = oldInput ? oldInput.selectionStart : null, selEnd = oldInput ? oldInput.selectionEnd : null;
...
if (newInput) {
  if (draft) newInput.value = draft;
  if (hadFocus) { newInput.focus(); try { newInput.setSelectionRange(selStart, selEnd); } catch (e) {} }
}
```
The structural alternative, splitting banners and composer into separate children so the composer node is never replaced, would also keep IME composition and avoid re-creating the chips and button. It needs a check of any CSS that relies on #announcements direct children.

